  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warming and drying trend through Sunday; cool/cold valley lows \nwith patchy frost.\n\n-  Increased westerly winds on Monday.\n\nThe Northern Rockies has settled into a pattern of high pressure\nover the region. Essentially this means warm and dry afternoons,\nwith valleys cooling off over night. Higher and typically cooler\nvalleys will have the chance of getting to freezing on any given\nmorning. Winds in general, will be light and terrain driven and \nfor those that do not work in wildland fire, terrain driven means \nup slope/ up valley during the day and then down slope/ down \nvalley at night. Basically, the heating of mountain slopes during\nthe day and cooling overnight will drive the winds. \n\nWhat will cause changes? Weak disturbances moving through the\nridge. The models are indicating one such disturbance on Monday,\nwhich will lead to a slight uptick in winds with a more westerly\ninfluence.\n\nNext Friday, the majority of models are indicating a potential \nfor change. While a majority indicates some change, they are split\non what that change will look like. More details to come as\nvariability slowly decreases within model outcomes.",
